is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified both control plane and data plane across the AS boundary and found no fault. End-to-end reachability is healthy: topology-wide probes show 0% loss both ways between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2 (200.1.1.2). Targeted tests confirm this: pc1->pc2 and pc2->pc1 each returned 10/10 ICMP replies with TTL=62, consistent with a two-hop path via router1 and router2. Hosts have correct default gateways (pc1 via 195.11.14.1, pc2 via 200.1.1.1).

On the routers, BGP is up and exchanging the expected routes. Router1 installs 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2 (BGP), and router2 installs 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1 (BGP). “show bgp summary” on both sides shows the single eBGP neighbor Established, with prefixes received/sent as expected. FRR services are active (running) on both routers.

Mechanistically, if a return-path issue existed, pc2->pc1 probes would fail and/or router2 would lack a route to 195.11.14.0/24. Neither is the case. Localization checks on router interfaces and routing tables show symmetric, correct forwarding.

Root cause: No current network fault; the reported one-way connectivity issue appears to be transient or unrelated to routing.

Corrective fix: No restart is required. The network is operating normally. If users see intermittent problems, capture timestamps/flows and consider checking application-layer or host firewall settings; otherwise, continue monitoring.
=== END ===